<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<schema xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema" xmlns:diggs="http://diggsml.org/schemas/3"
    xmlns:gml="http://www.opengis.net/gml/3.2"
    xmlns:vc="http://www.w3.org/2007/XMLSchema-versioning" vc:maxVersion="1.1" vc:minVersion="1.0"
    elementFormDefault="qualified" targetNamespace="http://diggsml.org/schemas/3" version="3.1.0">

    <annotation>
        <documentation>Schema for DIGGS Specification Registries.

            A specification registry is a standalone, authority-controlled document holding one
            canonical diggs:Specification per published standard - an ASTM or AASHTO method, a
            national or European standard, an agency special provision - so that a data provider
            cites a standard by reference rather than retyping its bibliographic details into every
            instance document. It is an authority artifact of the same kind as a code list
            dictionary, NOT project data: registries are published and versioned by DIGGS (or
            another named authority), never assembled inside a project's own instance document.

            WHY THIS EXISTS. diggs:Specification's own documentation (core/Common.xsd) states that
            it "exists to CITE a published standard - its properties are standardReferenceNumber,
            standardTitle, standardVersion and so on - and it states no requirement of its own."
            Until now there was nowhere to put a canonical one, so every provider inlined their own,
            and the same standard was described a dozen different ways. That variation is what a
            registry removes.

            THE CENTRAL DESIGN CONSTRAINT: A REFERENCE RETURNS EXACTLY WHAT AN INLINE VALUE WOULD.
            Almost every xlink:href in DIGGS resolves to the same object a consumer would have found
            inline - most urn:x-gml:targetElement declarations name a CONCRETE element
            (diggs:Project, diggs:Sample, diggs:Equipment, and diggs:Specification itself), not an
            abstract head. A receiving application that resolves diggs:governingStandard has always
            found a diggs:Specification there, and must continue to.

            So the registry does NOT extend or subtype diggs:Specification, and does not introduce
            any new element that could appear where a Specification is expected. It holds PLAIN
            diggs:Specification objects, unchanged and unwrapped, exactly as an instance document
            would carry them. An earlier draft of this schema defined a RegisteredSpecification
            substituting diggs:Specification; that was withdrawn precisely because it would have
            handed consumers an object of a type they had never seen at that position.

            The registry metadata therefore lives on a SEPARATE object -
            diggs:SpecificationRegistryEntry - which contains the Specification it registers. This
            is also the more honest modelling: registryStatus, authority and occurrences are facts
            about the REGISTRATION, not about the standard. Whether ASTM has withdrawn a method is a
            property of the standard's lifecycle as DIGGS catalogues it; it is not one of the
            bibliographic properties that identify the document.

            HOW A CITATION IS VALIDATED. Every property typed diggs:SpecificationPropertyType
            carries gml:AssociationAttributeGroup, so an instance may write

            &lt;governingStandard xlink:href="https://diggsml.org/def/registry/DIGGS/0/specifications.xml#astm_d1143"/&gt;

            with no schema change of any kind, and the href resolves to a plain diggs:Specification.
            Validation of that reference has two layers, and both reuse machinery that already
            exists:

            1. TARGET CLASS - the citing element carries
            &lt;appinfo source="urn:x-gml:targetElement"&gt;, the established DIGGS convention (46 uses
            across the schema; diggs:AbstractAcceptanceCriterionType/sourceSpecRef already names
            diggs:Specification this way). Because a registry holds plain Specifications, that
            declaration is satisfied directly - no substitution reasoning required of the consumer
            or the validator.

            2. TARGET APPLICABILITY - the question "is this citing element allowed to cite THIS
            standard?" is answered by the diggs:occurrences property on the registry ENTRY, reusing
            diggs:OccurrenceArrayPropertyType from Dictionary_diggs.xsd unchanged. Having resolved
            an href to a diggs:Specification inside a registry, a validator reaches the governing
            declarations by one deterministic step up:

            ancestor::diggs:SpecificationRegistryEntry/diggs:occurrences

            An entry declares the XPath(s) at which its Specification may legitimately be cited, so
            a laboratory sample preparation standard cannot be cited as a rigid inclusion design
            basis. This is the identical mechanism, in the identical shape, that
            dictionary-validation.xsl already applies to a code list Definition - the constraint
            travels with the registered term, so the validator stays generic and never needs
            per-domain knowledge or a lookup table.

            The one asymmetry with a code list is deliberate: a dictionary carries occurrences ON
            the object the codeSpace resolves to, whereas a registry carries them one level above
            the object the href resolves to. That single extra step is internal to the validator and
            costs nothing; keeping the href's return type stable is worth far more, because it
            affects every receiving application rather than one XSL module.

            SCOPE. ONE UNIFIED REGISTRY, NOT ONE PER DOMAIN. An earlier draft of this schema scoped
            each registry document to a single domain, mirroring how def's dictionaries are already
            organized by domain prefix (gp_, pil_, mwd_, cpt_, gr_inj_, ri_design_). That was
            withdrawn: a standard is routinely cited by more than one domain (ISO 22476-4 by both
            rigid inclusion design and in-situ testing; EN 12699/EN 14679 by both rigid inclusion
            design and construction execution), and per-domain files would have forced a choice
            between registering the same real-world standard twice under two different gml:ids - the
            exact ambiguity a registry exists to remove - or an arbitrary, undiscoverable "home" file
            for a cross-cutting standard. diggs:SpecificationRegistryEntry/diggs:domain
            (below) carries the classification instead: one registry, one entry per standard, one or
            more domain codes per entry. A rendering stylesheet filters by domain for browsability;
            the schema itself does not partition by it.</documentation>
    </annotation>

    <import namespace="http://www.opengis.net/gml/3.2" schemaLocation="core/gml3.2Profile_diggs.xsd"/>

    <!-- ==================== Registry root ==================== -->

    <element name="SpecificationRegistry" type="diggs:SpecificationRegistryType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>The root element of a specification registry document. Holds the
                registry's own identity - gml:identifier carrying the registry's canonical URL,
                gml:name, gml:description - inherited from gml:AbstractGMLType, followed by its
                entries.

                A registry document is validated against Diggs.xsd like any other DIGGS document;
                this element is its root, in the same way gml:Dictionary is the root of a code list
                document. It is NOT reachable from diggs:Diggs and must never be embedded in a
                project instance: a registry is published by an authority, and a project cites it.

                ONE UNIFIED REGISTRY IS THE INTENDED SHAPE, NOT ONE PER DOMAIN - see this schema's
                own top-level SCOPE note for why a per-domain split was tried and withdrawn. Say what
                the registry as a whole covers in gml:description; say what one ENTRY covers with its
                own diggs:domain classification (one or more codes) plus its diggs:occurrences
                declarations, which are what actually enforce where a given standard may be
                cited.

                ORDER ENTRIES SO THAT A REFERENCE FOLLOWS ITS ANTECEDENT. Where one entry's
                supersededByRef points at another, the superseding entry must appear first, as
                DIGGS forbids forward references throughout.</documentation>
        </annotation>
    </element>

    <complexType name="SpecificationRegistryType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>Type for the root of a specification registry. Extends
                gml:AbstractGMLType so that a registry carries gml:id, gml:identifier, gml:name and
                gml:description exactly as a gml:Dictionary does - the registry's own citable
                identity, distinct from the identity of the standards it holds.</documentation>
        </annotation>
        <complexContent>
            <extension base="gml:AbstractGMLType">
                <sequence>
                    <element maxOccurs="unbounded" minOccurs="0" name="registryEntry"
                        type="diggs:SpecificationRegistryEntryPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>One entry of the registry, containing exactly one
                                diggs:SpecificationRegistryEntry. Repeatable; a registry with no
                                entries is permitted so that a domain registry can be published
                                before it is populated.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                </sequence>
            </extension>
        </complexContent>
    </complexType>

    <complexType name="SpecificationRegistryEntryPropertyType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>Property type containing one diggs:SpecificationRegistryEntry.

                Deliberately carries NO gml:AssociationAttributeGroup. A registry OWNS its entries -
                they are held by value, never by reference - and permitting an xlink:href here would
                allow a registry to be assembled by pointing at entries held elsewhere, which
                defeats the point of a single authority-controlled document. Ownership is
                expressible, association is not.</documentation>
        </annotation>
        <sequence>
            <element ref="diggs:SpecificationRegistryEntry"/>
        </sequence>
        <attributeGroup ref="gml:OwnershipAttributeGroup"/>
    </complexType>

    <!-- ==================== Registry entry ==================== -->

    <element name="SpecificationRegistryEntry" type="diggs:SpecificationRegistryEntryType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>One registration in a specification registry: the canonical
                diggs:Specification for a standard - or for a named part, clause or procedure of a
                standard - together with the metadata that governs how it may be cited.

                This object exists so that the registered diggs:Specification can stay exactly what
                it always was. It substitutes nothing and appears nowhere except inside a registry,
                so no consuming application ever meets it where it expected a Specification. A
                reference into a registry resolves to the contained Specification, not to this
                entry.

                CHOOSING gml:id ON THE CONTAINED SPECIFICATION. The Specification's gml:id is the
                citation token - it is what appears after the '#' in every xlink:href - so it should
                track the standard's own identifier as closely as XML permits. gml:id is an XML ID
                and therefore an NCName: it may not begin with a digit and may not contain a space,
                a slash or a colon. So "ASTM D1143" becomes astm_d1143 and "BS 8006-1:2010" becomes
                bs_8006_1_2010. Use lower snake case throughout, and keep the token stable forever
                once published - a registry whose identifiers churn is worse than no registry,
                because existing citations silently break.

                SUBMETHODS. A named procedure within a standard is its own entry, not a variant of
                one: ASTM D1143 Procedure A gets its own SpecificationRegistryEntry whose
                Specification carries its own gml:id (astm_d1143_proc_a), repeats the parent's
                standardReferenceNumber, and is distinguished by standardClause, standardPart or
                shortMethodName. Because those are structured properties rather than free text, a
                consumer can group every Specification sharing a standardReferenceNumber under its
                parent standard mechanically. That grouping is the only reason to prefer registering
                a submethod over inventing an ad hoc code for it.</documentation>
        </annotation>
    </element>

    <complexType name="SpecificationRegistryEntryType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>Type for one registration. Extends diggs:AbstractObjectType, the standard
                base for DIGGS component objects, since an entry has no independent existence
                outside the registry that contains it.

                Holds the registered diggs:Specification by value, followed by the registration
                metadata. Every metadata property is optional except the Specification itself and
                its domain classification: an entry that registers nothing is meaningless, an entry
                with no declared domain cannot be found by a domain-scoped consumer and defeats the
                reason domain exists, but an entry that declares no lifecycle, authority or scope
                beyond that is merely unrestricted.</documentation>
        </annotation>
        <complexContent>
            <extension base="diggs:AbstractObjectType">
                <sequence>
                    <element maxOccurs="1" minOccurs="1" name="specification"
                        type="diggs:RegisteredSpecificationPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>The registered standard, as a plain diggs:Specification.
                                This is the object an xlink:href into the registry resolves to, and
                                it is bit-for-bit the same kind of object an instance document would
                                otherwise have carried inline - which is the whole point of holding
                                it here rather than subtyping it.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element maxOccurs="unbounded" minOccurs="1" name="domain" type="gml:CodeType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>One or more classifications of which domain(s) of
                                practice cite this standard - rigid inclusions, laboratory testing,
                                in-situ testing, sampling, construction, and so on. Coded against
                                def/docs/codes/DIGGS/0.1/specificationDomain.xml.

                                THIS IS WHY ONE REGISTRY CAN SERVE EVERY DOMAIN. A standard cited by
                                more than one domain - ISO 22476-4, say, cited by both rigid
                                inclusion design and in-situ testing - carries one domain element per
                                domain rather than being registered a second time under a different
                                gml:id: the registry holds exactly one canonical entry per real-world
                                standard, never two ids for the same document. domain is what a
                                consumer or a rendering stylesheet filters and browses by, so a
                                registry spanning every domain stays as easy to search as a
                                single-domain one would have been.

                                Mandatory, unlike every other metadata property on this type: an
                                entry with no declared domain cannot be found by a domain-scoped
                                consumer, which defeats the reason domain exists.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="authority" type="string">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>Name of the organization that controls the registry entry
                                - normally DIGGS. Distinct from the Specification's own
                                accreditingBody, which names the body that publishes the STANDARD
                                (ASTM International, BSI, CEN). Mirrors
                                diggs:DefinitionType/authority.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="reference" type="anyURI">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>A URI for the standard itself - the publisher's landing
                                page or DOI - so a reader can reach the source document. Mirrors
                                diggs:DefinitionType/reference. This is where the standard LIVES,
                                not where the entry lives; the latter is the registry's own
                                gml:identifier plus the Specification's gml:id.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="registryStatus"
                        type="diggs:RegistryStatusEnumType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>Lifecycle state of the registered standard. A registry
                                must be able to say that a standard has been withdrawn or superseded
                                WITHOUT deleting its entry, because instances published while it was
                                current still cite it and those citations must keep resolving. ASTM
                                D1194 is the worked example: withdrawn, but present in the
                                historical record of plate load testing.

                                Absent means active; state it explicitly for anything that is
                                not.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element minOccurs="0" name="supersededByRef"
                        type="diggs:FeatureReferenceType">
                        <annotation>
                            <appinfo source="urn:x-gml:targetElement">diggs:Specification</appinfo>
                            <documentation>For an entry whose registryStatus is superseded or
                                withdrawn, a reference to the Specification that replaces it -
                                normally one registered elsewhere in the same registry. Lets a
                                consumer follow a historical citation forward to the current
                                standard.

                                Points at the replacement diggs:Specification, not at its
                                SpecificationRegistryEntry, so that following this reference yields
                                the same kind of object as following a governingStandard. The
                                superseding entry must appear EARLIER in the registry than the entry
                                that cites it; DIGGS forbids forward references.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                    <element maxOccurs="1" minOccurs="0" name="occurrences"
                        type="diggs:OccurrenceArrayPropertyType">
                        <annotation>
                            <documentation>One or more diggs:Occurrence objects declaring where the
                                registered Specification may legitimately be cited:
                                sourceElementXpath gives the element that may hold a reference to
                                it, and the optional conditionalElementXpath gives an element (and
                                optionally a value) that must be present for the citation to be
                                valid.

                                THIS IS THE DOMAIN CHECK. Resolving an xlink:href proves only that
                                the target exists and is a diggs:Specification; it cannot tell a
                                rigid inclusion design basis from a laboratory sample preparation
                                method, since both are Specifications. These declarations are what
                                make the distinction machine-checkable, and they are read from the
                                target's entry rather than configured into the validator - so the
                                validator stays generic and vocabulary-independent, exactly as
                                dictionary-validation.xsl already is for code lists.

                                A validator that has resolved an href to a Specification inside a
                                registry reaches these by one step up:
                                ancestor::diggs:SpecificationRegistryEntry/diggs:occurrences.

                                An entry with no occurrences declares no restriction, and its
                                Specification may be cited anywhere one is permitted. Prefer stating
                                them: an unbounded entry is the registry equivalent of an
                                unconditioned dictionary Occurrence, and those have been a recurring
                                source of misuse.

                                Reuses diggs:OccurrenceArrayPropertyType from Dictionary_diggs.xsd
                                without modification. The shape is not dictionary-specific - it is a
                                general "this term is permitted here, conditionally on that" - and
                                reusing it rather than inventing a parallel form is what lets one
                                validator serve both mechanisms.</documentation>
                        </annotation>
                    </element>
                </sequence>
            </extension>
        </complexContent>
    </complexType>

    <complexType name="RegisteredSpecificationPropertyType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>Property type containing one plain diggs:Specification, as held by a
                registry entry.

                Deliberately carries NO gml:AssociationAttributeGroup, for the same reason
                diggs:SpecificationRegistryEntryPropertyType does not: a registry holds its
                Specifications by value. If this permitted xlink:href, a registry could register a
                standard it does not itself contain, and the canonical copy would stop being
                canonical.

                Distinct from diggs:SpecificationPropertyType (core/Common.xsd), which DOES carry
                the association group - that is what lets an instance document cite a registry entry
                by reference in the first place.</documentation>
        </annotation>
        <sequence>
            <element ref="diggs:Specification"/>
        </sequence>
        <attributeGroup ref="gml:OwnershipAttributeGroup"/>
    </complexType>

    <!-- ==================== Enumerations ==================== -->

    <simpleType name="RegistryStatusEnumType">
        <annotation>
            <documentation>Lifecycle states of a registered specification. Closed, because the three
                states are exhaustive: a standard is current, replaced by a named successor, or
                retired without one.</documentation>
        </annotation>
        <restriction base="string">
            <enumeration value="active">
                <annotation>
                    <documentation>Current and in force. The default reading when registryStatus is
                        absent.</documentation>
                </annotation>
            </enumeration>
            <enumeration value="superseded">
                <annotation>
                    <documentation>Replaced by a later standard, which should be named in
                        supersededByRef. The entry is retained so existing citations keep
                        resolving.</documentation>
                </annotation>
            </enumeration>
            <enumeration value="withdrawn">
                <annotation>
                    <documentation>Withdrawn by its publisher without a direct replacement. Retained
                        for the same reason: instances published while it was current still cite
                        it.</documentation>
                </annotation>
            </enumeration>
        </restriction>
    </simpleType>

</schema>
